==Harris==
by Edward Cree
--Aircraft Designer--

If a new game is started with the "Game with Builder" main-menu option, the available bomber types, instead of those historically supplied to Bomber Command, will be models developed by the player through the "builder" subsystem.

-Research-
The game begins with a standard 1939 technology base, allowing for monoplane medium bombers with cantilever wings, powered gun turrets, internal bomb bays and up to three engines.  To advance beyond this and permit the development of more capable aircraft, there is a technology tree which unlocks new parts and improves various coefficients.  However, you don't get to choose exactly what gets researched when.  Instead, at the start of each month, you choose three techs to fill your 'slots'; at the end of the month, one of those three will be selected at random to unlock.
Techs have a defined date at which they become available; normally, you cannot choose a tech until this date, but if you have two slots free, you can fill one with a tech from up to six months in the future, and the other with "Supporting Research".  At the end of the month, if the tech is selected it will be unlocked as normal and both slots freed; if Supporting Research is selected then the tech will become available to choose without Supporting Research, as though its date had already arrived.
You can leave a slot empty; if that slot gets selected, you will get a boost to your budget (£4,800 per day in 1939, increasing by £400 per day each year).  Like any other cash source, this will decay over time (£4,800/day sums to £120k in the limit); you're usually better off researching an actual tech.
Many techs have prerequisites; besides other techs, some techs depend on 'special' prerequisites: advanced geodetics techs can only be developed once you have flown a prototype with Geodetic fuselage, while improvements to four-engined bomber design require you to fly a four-engined prototype before you can start researching them.
The research screen, which appears automatically at the start of each month to let you choose your three techs, can also be opened at any time with the "Meet Researchers" button in the control screen, although you won't be able to change your slots unless it's still the first day of the month.  Besides available, selected, and near-future techs, this screen also shows the 'Research Archive' of already-unlocked techs.  Clicking on any tech will display some information about it, including a short description and a complete table of all its effects — be that engines or turrets unlocked, or coefficients changed.  In the latter case, the green column will show the coefficient as of that tech, and the red column will show the current coefficient (which will generally be worse if you're looking at an un-researched tech, whereas if you're looking at the Research Archive it'll usually be the same, or may even be better if you've unlocked another relevant tech since this one).

-Manufacturers-
There are eight aircraft manufacturers in the game: Armstrong Whitworth, A.V.Roe & Co. ("Avro"), the British & Colonial Aircraft Co. ("Bristol"), de Havilland, Handley Page, Short Brothers, Supermarine, and Vickers.  Each has its own traits, which may make its aircraft better or worse; as a rule, every manufacturer has something they do better than the others, so depending on what kind of bomber you're building, any one of them could be best.
Each design of bomber belongs to whichever manufacturer developed it; all subsequent marks of, and modifications to, that type must be developed by the same manufacturer.  Thus, you may want to plan ahead, using your second-choice manufacturer now so that the best one isn't too busy to work on your next design.
Once the drawings for a design are issued (be it a brand-new type or a mark or mod of an existing type), it must be prototyped and tooled for production before you can use it; both processes take time and money.  Each manufacturer's design office can work on only one prototype at a time; similarly, their production engineers can only work on tooling for one design (not necessarily the same one!)  You can, however, halt work on one design to switch them over to another, then later resume the first design from where you left off — progress is not lost.
The exact performance of a design is not known until the prototype is completed and test-flown; however, if you choose not to start spending money on tooling until you know the design is a good one, the eventual service entry will be much later than if you 'order it off the drawing board' and run both processes in parallel.
The manufacturers' screen can be opened with the "Meet Manufacturers" button in the control screen, and also opens automatically whenever you issue drawings for a new design.  It shows the eight manufacturers and each of their designs, with a colour-coded marker — red for a paper project, yellow for a flown prototype, or green for a design that's been fully tooled for mass production.  The marker may be extended with chevrons to show that prototyping or tooling is currently in progress.
Clicking on one of these designs will show its details, including vital statistics, performance, and costings.  Buttons 'Prototype', 'Tool', and/or 'Halt work' allow you to start or stop development work on an in-progress design, while further buttons let you commence a Mark or Mod refit of a completed design or start drawing up a completely new design using an existing one as a starting-point.
The "Show:" toggles alter what performance figures are displayed: if "Current doctrine" is disabled then calculations will use the doctrine as of the time the design was issued, while "Test results" shows either the prototype as-flown (enabled) or the original predictions made by the drawing-office (disabled).

-Builder-
The builder, or Aircraft Design screen, is opened either with the "Design Aircraft" button in the control screen, or any of the "use as basis" buttons in the manufacturers' screen.  Here, you can specify all the leading particulars of your new aircraft type with the controls on the left-hand side, while watching how the predicted performance changes on the right.  When you're happy with your design (and there are no critical design errors that prevent it from being built), click "Issue drawings" to create the design; this will take you to the manufacturers' screen, where you can start prototyping and tooling it.

Manufacturer: Choose one of the eight manufacturers.  The short description will give some indication of the selected manufacturer's strengths and weaknesses; some manufacturers also have an affinity with an engine supplier (Armstrong Whitworth for Armstrong Siddeley, and Bristol for their own engines) which will reduce the tare weight of engine mounts when using a matching engine type.

Engines: Choose the type and number of engines.  Besides horsepower, engines vary in their cost, tare weight, drag, reliability and serviceability, vulnerability to damage, and even how strongly supercharged they are for high-altitude performance.  (There are three supercharging levels: one-speed superchargers start to lose power above about 10,000ft, while two-speed superchargers can switch into "F.S. gear", using 2% of the engine's shaft power to maintain maximum boost up to 16,000ft; the two-speed two-stage supercharger, found only on the Merlin 60, has a full-throttle height of 12,000ft in M.S. gear, while F.S. gear takes 6% of the shaft power but keeps full boost as high as 21,000ft, allowing some impressively high-flying aircraft designs).  Some engines (the Vulture, Sabre and Centaurus) are so large that even a twin-engined bomber using them will be classed as a 'heavy'; otherwise, four or more engines qualify a design for this flag.
Some engines have another specific engine type that's considered their 'upgrade'; if you select 'Overbuild mounts' then a later Mod refit can swap in the upgraded engines (this also means that a Mark refit using them is faster and cheaper to develop), but until then you're paying a weight and cost penalty for the extra structure.
If you use an odd number of engines, the nose-mounted engine will produce slightly less effective power owing to slipstream drag on the fuselage, and also prevent the use of nose turrets.
The most reliable aircraft are twin-engined, since they can (unless badly damaged) continue to fly with one engine out, and adding more engines just increases the chance of breakdowns.  A single-engined aircraft is much more susceptible to mechanical failure, since if it loses that one engine it's dead.

Wing: Choose the area and aspect ratio.  A larger wing is heavier, but will reduce the take-off speed (important because grass and concrete runways each have a maximum allowed speed, and if a bomber exceeds this then bombs will be offloaded until it fits) and increase manœuvrability (lower wing loading improves turn rate), as well as having more room to fit fuel tanks.  High aspect ratio wings increase weight and cost, reduce manœuvrability (via roll rate), and are less resilient to damage, but their higher lift/drag ratios improve speed, range, and ceiling altitude.  Low aspect ratio slightly increases take-off speed.

Fuselage: Choose from up to four fuselage types.  "Normal" is the baseline design; "Slender" reduces weight and drag, but with penalties to vulnerability, reliability, and serviceability; it's also slightly more expensive to manufacture, and is incompatible with tail turrets (because the tail boom is too small to fit a man inside.  Look at a picture of a Hampden and you'll get the idea).  "Slabby" is cheap to build and even lighter-weight than Slender, brings bonuses to serviceability and reliability, and is required for the primitive pre-war design of quad-gun rear turret — but it brings a major increase in drag.  (For an idea of what this looks like, picture a Whitley or Stirling.)  "Geodetic" is only available for Vickers designs; this robust structure improves serviceability, reliability, and especially vulnerability, but it's the heaviest option and also the most expensive to manufacture.

Bomb bay: Choose the capacity (in lbs of GP bombs), and one of up to three 'girths'.  "Small" can only fit GPs and incendiaries, while "Medium" allows the carriage of sea mines and (once developed) 4,000lb High Capacity blockbuster bombs, although the bombdoors cannot be fully closed around such a large bomb and thus the aircraft's drag will be increased.  "Cookie" yields a large unobstructed bomb bay which can fully enclose 4,000lb or even larger HC bombs.  However, the wider bomb bays have tare weight penalties; there is an additional weight penalty for bomb bays above 8,000lb capacity, increasing the bigger you go (for Avro this penalty only starts at 9,000lb).
If you select 'CSBS', the aircraft will carry the improved Mark XIV Course Setting Bomb Sight, improving accuracy; it weighs 70lb more than the old Mark IX and costs an extra £1,200.

Turrets: Choose gun mountings in each of seven positions.  Each turret type has a certain amount of firepower and lookout coverage, which helps improve defence against fighters; but guns also add weight, cost, and drag, and even impact serviceability.  And, of course, they need a crew member to operate them.
Guns in the nose position cover the forward quadrant, one of the least important directions as night bombers are rarely attacked head-on; the Nose turret also covers the high beam.  They may be operated by a bomb-aimer (or in the case of the Ngun flex mount, by a navigator) rather than a dedicated gunner.  They can't be fitted to a bomber with an odd number of engines.
Dorsal turrets cover the upper angles, abeam and behind; these are fairly important.  There are also "dorsal after" flex mounts which are lighter and less draggy but don't cover the beam.  Guns in dorsal positions may be operated by a wireless-op.
Tail turrets cover the rear, both high and low.  The low rear is the most important direction, as nightfighters most often attack "von unten hinten": from below and behind.
Waist guns, flex mounts on either side of the fuselage, cover the beam quadrants, both high and low, although the early Beam mount has poor coverage downwards.  They may be operated by a wireless-op.
Ventral turrets cover the underside of the bomber — abeam, behind, and straight downwards.  The downwards direction becomes important after the Germans start fitting "Schräge Musik" upward-firing cannon to their nightfighters.  There are also "ventral after" flex mounts, similar to the "dorsal after" mounts; they cover the low beam and low rear but not straight downwards.  Guns in ventral positions may be operated by a wireless-op.
Chin turrets are periscopically-sighted guns underneath the bomber's nose, firing aft; they cover only the low rear and have poor sighting.  They may be operated by a navigator.
Fixed forward-firing guns, mounted in the wings, are operated by the pilot; they are lightweight and do not cause drag, but only provide limited head-on coverage.
Most bombers are required to carry guns; the Air Ministry will only allow unarmed single-engined bombers or, after the Unarmed Bomber tech has been unlocked, twins (and neither Ngun nor the fixed forward guns count).
You can right-click on a turret type to overbuild the mounting structure sufficient for that type, allowing it to be installed in a subsequent Mod refit.  Adding or changing a turret in a Mark refit is also faster and cheaper to develop if the mounts are not changed.

Crew: Choose how many of each type of crew to carry, and whether they should operate a gun position in addition to their main rôle.
You must have at least one Pilot; additional pilots increase the roll rate (and the max of all pilots' skill is used for calculating chance to evade fighter fire, and for airmanship if there's no engineer).
You must have at least one Navigator; additional navigators improve accuracy.
Bomb-aimers improve accuracy; the effect is smaller than another nav, but bomb-aimers are much easier to train (and can operate Nose turrets).
Engineers improve damage-control, reducing vulnerability; the first engineer also improves reliability.  Also, a highly skilled engineer can achieve better speed, altitude and fuel efficiency ("airmanship skill") than the official figures.  Only heavy bombers may carry an engineer.
Wireless-operators improve damage-control, though by less than an engineer; if the bomber has at least High Voltage electrics, they also improve accuracy, though by much less than a nav or bomb-aimer.  Their big advantage is that they can operate guns in the fuselage midsection (dorsal, ventral, waist).
Gunners' only job is manning their gun positions.  Besides firing the guns, they are also lookouts; the average skill of all gunners (and any W/ops or dual-rôling bomb-aimer-gunners) affects the chance to evade fighter fire.  If you have too few gunners to operate all your turrets, defence against fighters will suffer.

Electrics: Choose the bomber's electrical supply level.  "Low Voltage" is cheap but only runs basic systems; "High Voltage" allows early navigational aids including GEE to be fitted, while the yet more expensive "Stable Voltage" is needed for the advanced navaids.  Increasing electrical supply level also improves accuracy.  High and Stable both cost more if you have fewer engines, and Stable is extra-expensive to fit into a Slender fuselage.
Choose which navaids to fit.  Each increases the cost but provides its own benefits to navigation; see the main manual (the file 'HOWTOPLAY') for details.  ('FP' is FISHPOND, which is not really a navaid but an addon to H2S.)

Fuel: Choose the capacity of the fuel tankage in lb, and a fill percentage to be used in calculating the all-up weight.  (The tanks will automatically be filled beyond this, and bombs offloaded to meet weight and take-off speed limits, if the bomber is send on longer-range missions which require it.)  The resulting fuel load will determine the endurance and thus, in combination with cruise speed, the range.  Squeezing lots of fuel into a small wing increases vulnerability as fires are more likely.
If you select 'SST', the bomber will be fitted with Self-Sealing Tanks.  These have a slight weight and cost penalty, but greatly reduce vulnerability.

Max. gross take-off weight: This will normally be calculated automatically, but you can set it higher to allow for future growth (e.g. to make use of increased take-off speeds when runway techs are unlocked).  Doing so will increase the production cost of the bomber's structure.
Bombers with a gross weight below 21,000lb get a "light bomber" bonus to accuracy, reflecting their being easier to steer.  This bonus is 1 point per 1,500lb below the cutoff.
The mgtow controls the maximum number of crew that can fit inside the bomber; it must be at least 10,000lb for 3 crew, plus 5,556lb for each additional man.

-Refits-
You can develop a new Mark of an existing design, which reduces development time and cost relative to a clean-sheet design and allows squadrons and aircrew to switch over without retraining.  You can also develop a new Mod to apply to an existing mark, which is applied retroactively to existing airframes as well as to new production, and is generally even cheaper and quicker to develop.  However, refits are limited in what changes you can make, and not all improvements from research can be applied in refits (the coefficients shown in the research screen indicate the minimum level of refit they apply to).

The following cannot be changed in refits:
* manufacturer
* number of engines
* wing
* bomb bay capacity or girth
* fuselage type

The following changes can be made in marks but not mods:
* change engine type or mounts
* add new turrets or mounts
* add new crew
* change electric supply level
* change fuel capacity
* change max gross take-off weight

The following changes can be made even in mods:
* upgrade engine on previously overbuilt mounts
  - note that your fuelmass stays the same, so fuel in hours may decrease!
  - the engine must be the precise type the mounts were designed for
* remove turrets
* add turrets to existing mounts
  - the turret must be in the same location as the mount, and no heavier
* remove crew
* re-add crew that were originally present on this mark
* toggle CSBS
* add or remove navaids
* toggle SST
* change dual-rôling of crewmen
* change fuel fill level

-Strategy-
These are just some suggestions for things to build; it's worth exploring different possibilities and trying to come up with novel designs that work well (especially as knowing obscure corners of the design space will help when the RNG gives you a lopsided set of techs that prevents you from building the historical standards).
The legacy fleet will normally consist of twins; three-engine bombers are an option for heavier payloads or increased speed/altitude but they're usually too expensive to be worth it, while singles are horrendously unreliable and, with 1935 engines, can't get decent survivability with a meaningful payload/range.
Within those twins, a light bomber (around 1,000 to 1,500lb payload, girth SMA, Mercury engines) can achieve good accuracy and reasonable defence by virtue of speed and ceiling but that payload will only scratch at targets, range is short (you'll probably have to base it in 2 Grp to get any use out of it), and the accuracy bonus isn't enough to really score against hard-to-hit tactical targets or ships.  On the other hand, accuracy does help with Confidence (which in the early years is mostly driven by the fraction of sorties that hit).  Good choices of manufacturer include Bristol (can gain a point of Defence through better manœuvrability) and Supermarine (low drag boosts speed and range, which then allows you to shrink the fuel load for a cheaper, more survivable bomber).  A slabby fuselage will cut costs but the lower speed (and higher fuel load to get the range back) increases vulnerability, especially to fighters.  On the other hand, a slender fuselage is more easily damaged by flak but lets you build lighter and faster, boosting accuracy and defence against fighters.  (But then, fighters aren't really a serious threat early on.)  The best choice of armament to fulfil the Air Ministry's insistence on guns is Daf1 — it's about the lightest and cheapest turret and can be manned by W* who also gives damage control.  Even more weight can be saved by using a Chin turret as that allows a crew of just two, but dual-rôling the N to fire it cuts into accuracy.  Come 1938 you may want to upgrade this with Perseus engines; maybe even refit with the Taurus later if lights still look useful when Sleeve Valve unlocks.
At the heavier end, you can get payloads up to Whitley levels or even beyond on a pair of Tiger engines (generally better than Merlins here because of the lower vulnerability of air-cooling and the lower fuel consumption, plus they pair well with Armstrong Whitworth who give you mass savings on slab-sided fuselage and short stubby wings); a bombload as high as 10,000lb over short ranges (550mi or so) is achievable on about £8000 but at the cost of poor accuracy and a low-and-slow flight performance that gives atrocious defence against fighters (we're talking worse than 50) — and trying to fix that by piling on guns just weighs it down even more.  You're better off with a more reasonable payload that lets you fit in a bomb-aimer for better accuracy and maybe a tail turret and gunner.  You could even squeeze in a second W/Op for double the damcon to get better survivability against flak despite the low ceiling, but then you do have to train enough W/Ops so this is only wise if your other legacy types don't need them.  Unlike the light-bomber, this should definitely have girth MED as it's likely to do a lot of minelaying for cash and Confidence once flak makes flights over Germany too dangerous.
The most important type in your early-war arsenal is likely to be a geodetic medium from Vickers.  Since it's so crucial (with its far lower vulnerability, especially to flak, reducing loss rates), you probably want to wait until 1938 (at least) to build it, allowing you to design around better engines like the Pegasus (excellent power-to-weight, especially for a supercharged model) or the Merlin X (brings supercharging to the low-drag, reliable Merlin line, for a high ceiling and top speed — great for dodging fighters — but at the vulnerability cost of liquid cooling).  They will push up the cost, but, aren't the lives of your aircrews worth it?  Depending how worried you are about fighters, you might add a tail and even a nose turret, or take the opposite tack and just have an N*-operated Chin turret, relying on height and speed to protect you.  An important decision will be whether to carry a bomb-aimer; the extra accuracy is nice and he can man a nose turret, but that all pushes up cost and means you'll struggle to train up enough Bs early on (you only start with 150!) and will have to send a lot of B-crewed airframes to OTU if you want to be in a good state by the time the real bombing starts in May 1940.  It's wise to make payload at least 4,000lb and girth MED so that you'll have the option to use it as a cookie truck in 1942.
To get into the heavy game, you will need to research either Four Engined Bombers or one of the large engines (Sleeve Valve for the Centaurus, 24-Cylinder Engines for the Vulture and Sabre).  A heavy twin can be built small enough to operate effectively from improved grass runways (albeit with quite short range) and be quite cheap compared to the four-engined options, but the latter have better performance and a steady development path through engine upgrades (whereas the Vulture V is still unreliable crap, the Centaurus VII is contemporaneous with the excellent Hercules XVIII, and the Sabre V doesn't arrive until 1944).  A heavy will definitely want to carry an Engineer for the damage control and probably also a Bomb-aimer to apply its great load more accurately on the industrial targets it'll mostly be busy with through 1941; it can be tempting to give your earliest heavy SMA girth to save weight (especially if you've researched Bomb Cells), but that does mean it won't get a second lease of life minelaying when it's obsoleted from the front line.  As for which engine to use on a FEB, the Hercules III is robust (being air-cooled) but pushes Failure rates uncomfortably high — though the XI upgrade will likely become available not long after the original design finishes tooling.  The Merlin X is much more reliable (giving less than half the Failure rate) and the lower drag means longer range (or a smaller fuel load), but with less power you'll be lower and slower, increasing the chances of getting hit by flak to which your liquid-cooled engines are also more vulnerable.  Its upgrade (the Merlin XX) arrives about the same time, but is not as big an improvement as the Herc.  The Merlin is most favoured for a long-range type, where the Herc can require 20% more fuel to get the same range (the wing area having to increase too to keep take-off speed under 106mph) and thus the plane as a whole ends up costing more (also, longer flights mean the Failure stat is more important); but you're likely to wait a few months to develop things like Lightweight Wings and maybe Large Bomb Bay before you try to create a heavy suitable to keep using well into the Area Bombing era.  If you really want to get weird, 5 or even 6 of a smaller engine could give higher performance while still fitting into the 70,000lb gross weight limit of concrete runways, but many of the smaller engines have only a single-speed supercharger, leaving only the Tiger and Pegasus as decent choices for a 6-engined monster (5 Merlins also just about works).  Or again three 2,000hp-class engines can perform passably well, but has the same development-path problem as the heavy twin.
As for choice of manufacturer for a heavy: Handley Page's reliability bonus can be valuable if you're using balky engines like the Hercules III or a pair of Vultures or Sabres.  Shorts build cheap and robust but heavy and the poor serviceability means you'll end up spending more on airframes for the same front-line strength (on the other hand, once they start going to HCU serviceability doesn't matter).  Avro are the kings of heavy-bomber design, with tare weight savings on the main features of the genre, but you probably want to save them for later so that you can build an Ideal Bomber with 1941 wing and bomb-bay techs to serve through the late Area Bombing campaign, without them having to flip back and forth between supporting an early heavy with upgrades and developing the new one.  Vickers could produce a geodetic heavy twin or trimotor with impressive survivability, but they're likely busy with upgrades to your main medium bomber; by the time that finally reaches retirement, developing Four-Engined Geodetics is also an option that could enable a successor to the Avro type, but while the robust geodetics improve defence against flak, the extra drag means they can't match the height and speed of conventional fuselages that evade fighters.  Supermarine could be a good choice for a design that won't otherwise take too long to build, such as a high-speed unarmed heavy twin; they and de Havilland are also the manufacturers of choice for unarmed medium bombers when the doctrine tech allowing for that is unlocked.
Planning ahead is made difficult by the way the research RNG works; if there's a tech you need, on *average* it will take three months to unlock, but it could take arbitrarily long — and if you didn't get it this month, then it's *still* an average of three months away (for the statisticians in the audience, that's because the geometric distribution is memoryless).  If you delayed a design because you wanted to incorporate the new tech, and it didn't come up, you've wasted that month, and your odds haven't even gotten any better; the sunk-cost fallacy can easily draw you into a frustrated loop of 'well let's try one more time'.  To succeed without relying on luck, you need flexible plans such that two or even three different techs all give you routes to achieve the capabilities your bombing campaign needs; for that reason, you can't just go all-in on a single golden design (say, a Wellington clone as your only medium bomber), instead you need multiple types with a variety of design philosophies (and using a variety of engines) so that you have a way to use whatever you get.
That said, not all techs are equally valuable.  Some, like Self-Sealing Tanks, are vital enough that your loss-rate and force-strength graphs will show a visible discontinuity at the point they're installed into your bombers; and Four Engined Bombers are generally superior to heavy twins.  Runway technologies are essential to grow beyond medium bombers (pre-war grass strips limit you to 50,000lb gross weight).  Hyperbolic Navigation unlocks not only GEE but also bomber stream tactics.  But there are some techs that are distinctly weak, like 24-Cylinder Engines (which gives you the Vulture, too unreliable to sensibly use, and the Sabre that's not much better, expensive as hell, and only single-speed supercharged) or Improved Ventral Turrets (there is very little point in the ventral turret until Schräge Musik comes along a year and a half later, although when it does IVT and its successor Ball Turret can be useful).  Bomb Cells can look nice but in the long term you'll wish your bombers could drop mines.  Turret Fairings, Improved Hydraulics, and especially Lightweight Ammo Tracks are almost new-player traps: the weight or drag savings they produce are very small (LAT only cuts 60lb from a twin turret!) and optimal designs mostly have few guns anyway — a heavily-armed bomber is usually only a little better against fighters than a fast and high-flying one, needs more crew, costs more to build, is harder to maintain, potentially requires stronger runways, and worst of all, is more vulnerable to flak.  (Remember that increasing speed doesn't just improve the bomber's Defence stats, it also makes it less likely to be intercepted in the first place, and gives flak fewer shots at it as it spends less time in range.  A bomber with a minimum of armament and a below-typical payload can actually be faster than German nightfighters — not just Mosquitoes, even something like 10,000lb on four Merlins can do it.)
If you are putting guns on a type, the tail turrets are the most effective, but need a dedicated gunner (which makes for a bigger, heavier, slower, costlier aircraft), which may make midsection guns (manned by W*) or a nose turret (B*) or even chin turret (N*) preferable.  For instance, Nose+Daft+Vent (which covers every direction, even Schräge) and a crew of PNB*W*W* can get better defn than Tail and a crew of PNBWG, although the extra drag lowers range and dual-rôling the B hampers accuracy.  It's worth looking at the data file defining the turrets (`builder/dat/guns`) to see each one's coverage directions, as two turrets covering the same quadrant are worth less than the sum of their parts — more generally, adding more guns to a quadrant has diminishing returns, so for instance twin turrets in the nose and tail achieve more overall than putting a quad turret in the tail and leaving the front and high beam unguarded.  And don't forget that the 'dice' (the randomisation that determines the performance when the prototype is test-flown) can change the trade-off of how extra weight and drag affect speed and ceiling, or whether a small gain in Defence is enough to change the integer to which the stat gets rounded.
If relying on H2S for navigation, you may find Schräge Musik difficult to deal with, as you can't fit a ventral turret to an H2S-equipped bomber.  Fortunately, shortly after the Germans start fitting the upward-firing cannon to their nightfighters, you can research Fishpond Display.  Adding FISHPOND equipment to your bombers is a quick and cheap mod that, by detecting aircraft below the bomber, provides some coverage to the lower quadrants (though not as much as a proper ventral turret which can shoot back).
